\subsection{Oracle Control Design}
\label{sec:oracle-design}

To isolate whether the bottleneck lies in constraint extraction or in the aggregation mechanism, we introduce an oracle control. For FOLIO \citep{han2022folio}, gold FOL annotations replace self-extracted constraints. For ProofWriter \citep{tafjord2021proofwriter}, gold domain-specific logic (DSL) rules serve the same role. In both cases, Z3 evaluates the gold constraints against each candidate answer produced by the original $K$ traces; the scoring function (Eq.~\ref{eq:score}) remains unchanged.

This design functions as a causal intervention on constraint quality. If the oracle variant produces large gains while self-extraction does not, the aggregation mechanism itself is sound, and the failure localizes to constraint extraction. Conversely, if the oracle also fails, the MAX-SAT aggregation framework is insufficient regardless of constraint quality. Results of this intervention appear in \S\ref{sec:oracle}.

\subsection{Scope}
\label{sec:scope}

SICA as evaluated here uses \emph{same-model self-extraction}: the same LLM generates traces and extracts constraints. This design isolates whether a model's own reasoning can be usefully formalized for aggregation. Methods that use trained extractors, external provers, or multi-model ensembles fall outside the scope of this evaluation. We test nine remediation strategies spanning prompting, architecture, capability, verification, diversity, and filtering dimensions (\S\ref{sec:remediation}).

\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:exp-setup}

We evaluate SICA against Self-Consistency (SC) across seven models from four base architectures (instruction-tuned, reasoning-specialized, and distilled variants) on four reasoning domains, with more than 2,400 total evaluation instances.

\paragraph{Models.} Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct, Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct, and Qwen3-14B (non-thinking mode)~\citep{yang2024qwen25}, Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.3~\citep{jiang2023mistral}, LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct~\citep{dubey2024llama3}, DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B~\citep{guo2025deepseekr1}, and QwQ-32B~\citep{qwen2024qwq}. Parameter counts range from 7B to 32B.

\paragraph{Datasets.} FOLIO~\citep{han2022folio} ($n=204$) provides formal logic problems with gold FOL annotations for the oracle control (\S\ref{sec:oracle}). ProofWriter~\citep{tafjord2021proofwriter} tests multi-step deductive reasoning ($n=600$). LogiQA~\citep{liu2020logiqa} evaluates logical reasoning ($n=200$). StrategyQA~\citep{geva2021strategyqa} covers commonsense reasoning ($n=200$ for LLaMA-3.1-8B; $n=60$ for Qwen2.5-14B). GSM8K~\citep{cobbe2021gsm8k} ($n=30$) and AIME 2024 ($n=22$) serve as operating-regime probes.

\paragraph{Parameters.} $K=12$ traces at $T=0.7$. Extraction at $T_{\text{ext}}=0.3$ (Appendix~\ref{app:prompts}). Z3~\citep{demoura2008z3} timeout: 10 seconds.

\paragraph{Metrics.} Accuracy (\%), $C_0$ (\S\ref{sec:c0}), and $\Delta = \text{SICA} - \text{SC}$ in percentage points (pp). McNemar exact test at $\alpha = 0.05$; 95\% bootstrap CIs (10{,}000 resamples) for $n \geq 200$.


\subsection{Main Results: A Regime-Dependent Effect}
\label{sec:main-results}

Table~\ref{tab:main_results} reports results across all conditions sorted by SC accuracy. SICA effectiveness depends on the model's baseline accuracy regime.

